<GuideLayout heading="Learn with a small, fictional study" lead="Try the workflow before bringing your own research into Fleuron. These guides use synthetic interviews and make the software’s limits explicit.">
  <h2><a href="/guides/code-interview-transcripts/">Code interview transcripts</a></h2>
  <p>Download two community-garden interviews. Follow a passage from import to coding, memo writing and export. Use the practice files to check whether Fleuron fits the way you work.</p>
  <h2><a href="/guides/reflexive-thematic-analysis/">Use an evolving analysis</a></h2>
  <p>Revisit an initial code, write down a competing interpretation and distinguish a tentative theme from a topic label. This guide discusses reflexive thematic analysis without claiming that a software preset supplies a method.</p>
  <h2><a href="/compare/free-qualitative-analysis-software/">Compare free qualitative software</a></h2>
  <p>Compare Fleuron with Taguette and QualCoder. Choose by the material you analyze, how you need to share it, installation requirements and export options.</p>
  <p class="note">Working with real interviews? Review your protocol and <a href="/privacy/">the data disclosure</a> first. The examples are fictional; your own documents may contain information that needs different handling.</p>
</GuideLayout>
